1.6.4.m:
Before the Sviṣṭakṛt offering, he offers six oblations of clarified butter:

"To him of a hundred weapons, of a hundred powers, / of a hundred aids, subduing the adversary;

May Indra lead us across every misfortune / through a hundred autumns!"

"Let these four paths, measuring out the space, / stretch between heaven and earth;

Whichever of them shall bring us imperishability, / to that one do ye deliver us, O All-Gods!"

"Spring, summer, the honey-rich rains, / autumn, and winter—may [they] establish [us] in well-being;

In the windless calm of these seasons of a hundred autumns, / may we be free from fear!"

"To the Saṃvatsara, to the Parivatsara, to the Idāvatsara, to the Anuvatsara, to the Udvatsara pay great homage!

In the goodwill of these worshipful ones / may we live long, unharmed!"

"This is the welfare of the Saṃvatsara, of the Parivatsara, of the Idāvatsara, of the Anuvatsara, of the Udvatsara;

May she, not being angered, convey us across; / through this day may I attain the [next] day!"

"May Agni consume first, / for he knows how the oblation [is];

May he who belongs to all peoples / make the plants auspicious for us!"—

with this [mantra] in the case of *śyāmāka* grain; in spring, in the case of bamboo barley.

1.7.2.m:
Having concluded the full-moon rite on the same day,
[with the verses]:
"Cosmic order indeed is Parameṣṭhin, nothing surpasses cosmic order;
on cosmic order the ocean is placed; by the ocean the earth is made firm;
Agni with sharp radiance, heat has been approached by the uṣṇih;
the head is placed in heat, in the splendour of Vaiśvānara;
Turn it with cosmic order! Reverse it with truth! Follow it with heat!
Bring it near with auspiciousness! Bring it near with strength!"
with a three-striped porcupine quill and an iron razor he turns around the hair and shaves the beard.

1.7.4.m:
Having brought the full-moon sacrifice to conclusion on the same day,

"That heat which rolled around, from the edge of the earth;

by that sacred formula of his I turn it away, so that he may live."

1.8.4.m:
With: 'O Jātavedas, go to the gods with the omentum; for you were the first hotṛ;

Unite, O Agni, with the body of clarified butter; may the desires of the sacrificer be true. Svāhā'—

having recited this, when the vaṣaṭ-call is uttered, he offers the omentum.

2.1.1.m:
'We have come here to the earth's place of sacrifice, / where all the gods found delight;

crossing over with ṛc, sāman, and yajus verses, / may we revel with prosperity of wealth and with food!'

With this verse, he occupies the sacrificial ground along with its roots.
